urate in any or all  these films that i've mentioned or that  i'm alluding to  i will say that there is a very strong  tendency by focusing on the transition  itself  to focus on the pain  and the problems in our lives  that come from that  whether it be  something like the danish girl where  lily literally died  trying to be  who she was  and  the film is largely about showing  the pain  of a male existence and using that as a  way to justify why  she took this chance or whether it be  something like boys don't cry  where  during the film brandon is largely in a  state of euphoria  about himself  it's still about the pain that's caused  when other people around him realize he  is trans similarly with  transamerica at the time that story  starts the league character has already  had her transition but it's about the  ripple effects and  the pain  caused by that  now basically any story is going to have  conflict and going to have characters  going through something that is painful  be it physical or emotional that's very  standard for any story but because the  transition itself is front and center  that means that these keeping depictions  of  trans-equaling  pain of some kind  physical pain dysphoric pain  pain of rejection from family  and  the prominence  of trans existence as pain  in narrative  i do feel at this point is becoming  legitimately harmful  while all the trans characters who are  supporting characters are helping to  counterbalance this because they don't  necessarily have to put their transition  front and center and are allowed to  simply be something else and just exist  as trans that certainly helps  but again every time we put the focus on  a trans person  look at the pain of being trans and like  i do understand that part of the reason  that pain has been put front and center  as a trans narrative is partly because  of the ways in which the trans community  itself has pushed for an understanding  there is a reason why there has been  historically and in  certain circles there still is such an  emphasis on dysphoria  because in trying to get cisgender  people who don't understand what it  feels like to be trans and it's not easy  for us to explain because there isn't  anything that you experience that we can  equate it to  linking it to  we are in pain because we're in the  wrong body  that was a way to  simplify and boil it down to something  that  was easier to push for  that was  well  it was transmedicalization  now the medicalizing of trans as a  concept the idea that only people who  have actually had surgery or  or who are on hormones or who have  dysphoria are the only people who are  legitimately trans and everyone else is  some kind of poser transtrender or  whatever is a harmful hurtful deplorable  narrative  but the initial pushing forward of  the pain and the dysphoria as a medical  concern  did  serve a purpose at one point  because that was something that we could  hopefully  get people who were not trans to  understand and if they treated it as a  medical condition with a medical  solution i.e hormones and or surgery  that was a start  but  that did unfortunately  put the pain of being trans front and  center  and did it for so long and so  effectively  that you see lots of people  when they start to question their own  gender  they fear the pain  that that life will bring  you see parents of trans kids  worried about the pain  that that existence will cause for them  you see them  focusing on the idea of their child  undergoing surgery this is a huge part  of the narrative pushing anti-trans  agendas the idea of they're trying to  give our kids these surgeries and  there's this focus on the medicalization  because there's this focus on the pain  and putting  trans pain  as  the forefront  of trans stories  in media  is feeding into that  because being trans  should not be defined by pain  and that is not to negate the pain of  any trans person  if you experience  severe dysphoria if it was only  alleviated by hormones and or by surgery  that is valid  that's also not me  i can't pretend to fully understand what  that's like  but  that is not the only thing that being  trans is  and i know it can be hard for some  people who feel like they have gone  through hell  to live as themselves  to hear people like me say okay but you  don't have to go through that to be  trans  i do understand that some of you did go  through that  but  those of us who didn't  are still trans  and again focusing  on the pain  be it something that political forces  focus on  the community itself focuses on  or media focuses on  you do that for too long  and that's what you frame being trans as  and that also  is not helping  and then  there's the offshoot of that i saved  this for the end because this is by far  not the main point i wanted to dwell on  partially because i've talked to this to  certain degrees before but this is  another element to it  and that is  the casting of cisgender people in trans  roles  as i said i've talked about it before  repeatedly but i feel like that's the  most relevant video if you don't  understand my basic premise but making  it as simple as i can  at this particular point in time  transgender people actors in particular  have a hard time getting roles at all  they are virtually never cast as cis  people and yet cis people are frequently  cast as trans people so trans people  can't even get casters themselves and  they don't get cast as anything else and  there is also the issue that casting a  cisgender person as a trans character  especially if the cisgender actor  their gender matches the gender that the  character transitioned out of instead of  into so in other words when you have a  man play a trans woman or a woman play a  trans man it contributes to the general  public perception that trans people are  really underneath whatever presentation  they give still the gender they were  assigned at birth and they're just  pretending because that's what the  actors are doing  it's not an intentional thing but it is  a persistent undercurrent that results  from that casting  here's why i bring it up again even  though i don't want to dwell on any of  the points you just made check out the  other video or other ones that i've made  on the topic since  the reason i bring it up again  is because i  at this point  the most common  thing i see brought up  trying to argue for the validity of  casting cisgender actors as transgender  characters  at least that gets deployed in good  faith because there's a ton of bad faith  versions the most common one being  directors they can be whatever you got  to insist we only cast aliens as alien  kids like all that's bad faith all of  that is bad faith and it's nonsense and  it is not actually engaging with any  point that i ever make it's  it is a thought-terminating cliche  but in terms of good faith concerns  the one that i see at this point  asks the following  wouldn't it be dysphoric for a trans  actor to play a pre-transition version  of that character isn't it a bad thing  to put an actor through that kind of  pain that's thing one  thing two  would it be believable would we be able  to pull off  believing the trans person as  the cisgender part of the story first  thing about both of these  it assumes that there has to be a  cisgender part of the story  and i think that's an offshoot of the  fact that this is the story that keeps  being told as the trans story  i've literally had comments be it here  on tik tok wherever i talk about this  where people present  concerns or questions that start from a  base assumption  that a trans story is going to deal with  a pre-transition period for that person  they assume that going in which  why wouldn't they assume that that's the  only kind of story that gets told  but let's actually address these two  things first one  the issue of  dysphoria  and again i do think  while this can be deployed in bad faith  any  valid argument can be deployed in bad  faith but i have seen it as a legitimate  concern from people  that  they would not want  a trans actor to  be put through a painful situation of  having to portray  something that isn't their gender  a couple of things about that  first is dysphoria is  incredibly personal  how severe it is  what sets it off  will vary wildly  from person to person  and while i do not doubt  there would be  some trans actors who would not be  comfortable portraying  the gender that they transitioned out of  i guarantee you that there's others who  would be who would be okay with it would  it be the most comfortable thing in the  world no not necessarily but actors  are frequently  asked to do things that are  uncomfortable i don't mean dangerous i'm  not talking about hazardous onset  conditions but  not trying to big up my own horn as a  pick up my own horn toot my own horn big  up myself mixing metaphors as an actor  um  but i got to be in a play  several years ago where i played a  character who was  deeply depressed and suicidal  and that was  very uncomfortable  like it left me in a bad place for a  little while playing that part but i  still volunteer because i wanted to do  it and i'm happy that i did it and i'm  proud of the work i did  when i performed that character on stage  but it was still uncomfortable  or if you want something more  recent just this year  in a fan audio adventure for doctor who  i did voice work  as a cis male character  and i was fine to do that  so while yes there will undoubtedly be  some trans people who would not be  comfortable portraying the gender that  they transitioned out of in their own  real life  there will be others who are okay with  that or at least okay enough with it to  be willing to take on the part  and to use that as a reason to just well  we can't cast trans person because of  this we have to cast a cis person no you  don't  maybe you have to look a little harder  to find the trans person who will be  good for the part and is comfortable  with that  but look  don't use it as an excuse to not look  and again this is actually an outcome  and a consequence of centering pain  as the central narrative of what it  means to be trans even people who are  trying to be allies  are  issuing concerns that would block trans  people trans performers from  opportunities because they are so  worried about pain and dysphoria because  that has become our entire  story  as for the other thing that one's really  easy anyone who says to me well  you know how would the trans person  portray you know the cis part of their  life well two things about that first is  being trans is not the same thing as  having had a transsexual operation and  actually having medical intervention to  change your gender  i have not had surgery i don't plan to  have surgery ever i am on hormones but  you can be trans while being on hormones  trans is who you are not necessarily all  the steps you have or plan to take to  best express that if you are able and  happy to fully express your sense of  gender  without hormones  without  surgery  you are just as trans as anybody else so  there's the first thing  uh  and then the second thing is well what  if they actually have had hormones or  surgeries or whatever how would you deal  with that well same reason same way you  deal with that in the other direction  same way you get the cis actors to look  trans the magic of hollywood makeup and  prosthetics  there's these weirdly  worded and overly  thought over and overly complicated  questions  that are very straightforward well what  if the actor's not comfortable with this  get a different actor  but but won't all trans people be like  no  well how do we make them look cis make  up  same way you make a cis person look  trans  and like i said this part of it was not  really the point  however i'd be lying if i didn't say  that some comments that i've seen about  this part of the topic  isn't why i wanted to talk about this  thing as a whole  right now the thing that that i want to  see more than anything  even more than seeing  trans actors play trans characters and  not see cis actors played in ortho i do  want that is to have  trans creators  being the ones telling these stories  i mentioned it in a short i did a week  or two back  right now these stories are being told  being written by being approved by in  the studios being directed by and being  acted in front of a screen by cisgender  people  our stories  are not in our own hands  and  funnily enough  more often than not when you get trans  creators even when they are telling  trans stories  it's usually about  a lot more than just being trans  you can just look at the wachowskis for  a primary example of that because if you  didn't realize the matrix is a trans  narrative or heck skip that just go to  sense8 and  not only is there a trans character in  there with as much prominence as any of  the other characters it being an  ensemble but the very nature of what  that story is functions in many ways as  a trans allegory just on premise  they can make it about being trans  without telling the standard trans  story  which is something that cisgender  creators can't seem to get past  and it's a story that's now been told  even if you think it's a story that's  been told well i would argue it's a  story that we don't need to keep telling  it's time to move on  it's time to show that being trans  is more than just the transition  that we are not defined by this one  aspect of who we are and that this one  aspect of who we are is not  necessarily defined as being  pain  those are my thoughts  on the state of  trans narratives in film  what do you think about this  whatever your thoughts are  drop something down in the comments and  let's talk about it  like share subscribe i've got a patreon  that is how i pay my bills and it's what  enables me to  be able to have these conversations with  you as regularly as i do  so  i hope you'll consider supporting me or  becoming a channel member if you want to  go that way  i'm not going to put massive pressure on  you though  because the thing i really want you to  remember  is that you are beautiful  you are valid  and you are loved  you are the council i'm just running the  meetings and until next time this  council  is adjourned  [Music]  hey if you're sticking around through  the credits might as well hear some  names read out my patreon helps support  this and in particular i want to thank  raven mcbain bookworm mj tracy scrabbit  vincent paul bartolucci kailyn schwartz  edelen robin moore rosholtz shayla  gourlay wanna hear me mispronounce your  name check out the rewards on the  patreon  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Music]  um  [Music]
 

